page=namespace sect=7 summary="per-process namespaces: names, mounts, binds and unions"
    keys=namespace,name,space,mount,bind,unmount,union,srv,post,group,template,newns,rfork

# DESCRIPTION

Every resource on VectraOS is a file tree served by some process, and every process sees the trees it has been given, arranged under names of its own choosing.
That arrangement is the process's namespace.
It is Plan 9's idea, as The Use of Name Spaces in Plan 9 describes it, kept whole and moved out of the kernel: the kernel has no paths, no mount table and no name cache, and a process's names are resolved by `lib/vx-ns`, in the process, over the 9Px connections it holds (ADR-0009).

A namespace is a table of mount points.
At each one is a union: one or more directories, each from a file server, tried in order.
`mount` puts a file server's tree on a name; `bind` puts a part of the namespace on another name; `unmount` takes either away.
Nothing else makes a name: there is no `/dev` the kernel supplies, no root file system it mounts, and no name a process can reach that it was not given.

Two processes may see the same names and different files, or the same files under different names.
A test sees `fsd`'s home branch at `/tmp`; the console shell sees a RAM file system there.
Neither is wrong: `/tmp` is what each was given.

Names are not authority.
A process can mount only a server it holds a handle to, so rearranging its own table gives it nothing it did not already have, and `bind` confines nothing.
A restricted view is a connection attached at a restricted root, which the server enforces; see {Authority|#authority}.

@node=names title="Names and how they are resolved"

A name is an absolute path of UTF-8 components, as utf(6) defines them.
It is cleaned before it is used, so `..` is taken away lexically and never climbs out of a bind, as in Plan 9.
A relative name is made absolute from the process's current directory before it is resolved (ADR-0039): one directory for the process, a path, which rc(1)'s `cd` and POSIX's `chdir` set and a child starts in.

Resolution walks from the root's mount, a walk(5) of the names that remain at a time.
A `Twalk` answers with one qid for each name walked, and vx-ns checks each against the table: a mount point is found by the identity of the directory it was made on, the connection and the qid path, not by the path it was made at.
At a mount point, the walk goes on from the root of the mount's union with the names that remain.
So a mount shows through every name that reaches its directory: after `bind /n/a /n/b` and a mount on `/n/a/m`, `/n/b/m` is the mount too, as in Plan 9.
Crossing a mount costs no extra round trip.

The path a mount or bind was made at is kept beside it, for ns(1).

@node=mount title="Mount and bind"

`mount` <srv> <old> [<aname>] attaches to a file server and puts the tree it serves on <old>.
The <srv> is a post, `/srv/`<name>, that the namespace already has a connection to, or a 9P server over TCP, `tcp!`<host>`!`<port>; <aname> chooses one of the server's trees, as fsd(4)'s branches are chosen, and is passed in the attach(5).
`bind` <new> <old> puts on <old> what <new> names now, the union there copied whole if it is one.
`unmount` [<new>] <old> takes one member away, or the whole union.

These are rc(1)'s builtins, with the same words as the lines of a namespace file, namespace(6), and as the lines ns(1) prints.
A native program makes the same changes through vx-ns.

A namespace holds at most 32 mount points, 8 members to a union and 16 servers.

@node=unions title="Unions"

A mount or bind onto a name that is already a mount point replaces what is there, or, with a flag, joins its union:

: `-b`
  The new directory goes before the members already there.
: `-a`
  It goes after them.
: `-c`
  Files created in the union are created in this member: the first member with `-c`, as 9front's `createdir` chooses.
  A union of more than one member with none refuses a create.

A walk into a union tries each member in order, and the first that has the name wins; a directory read lists every member's entries.
That is how `/bin` is made: the POSIX template binds `/boot/bin/posix` before the boot image's `/boot/bin`, so a POSIX program finds sbase's `ls` first and a native program, with only `/boot/bin`, finds the native one (see posix(7)).
A union's own directory stays its first member, so `ns` prints it as a bind of itself, as below.

@node=srv title="/srv and posting"

A file server reaches its clients through a post: a channel whose server end the server listens on and whose client end is kept under a name, `/srv/`<name>.
In Plan 9 `/srv` is a directory any process may post in.
Here the posts are made by svcd(8), from the `post=`, `claim=` and `connect=` keys of the service manifests (svc(6)), when it reads them at boot, and svcd keeps every client end.
A post is a rendezvous: a client may connect before its server has started, and waits.
svcd keeps a second handle to each server end too, so a client that connects while its server is restarting waits for it rather than failing.

`/srv/`<name> is a name in a namespace file, not a file: `/srv` is not a tree, and `ls /srv` fails (BUGS).
A process holds a connector to each post it was given, as a handle, and a mount of `/srv/`<name> is a connection through it; a process with no connector for a post cannot mount it, whatever its namespace file says.
In a namespace group, the connectors are kept by nsd(4) for the group, so any member can mount what one member has mounted.

The posts of a running system are its services': `/srv/cons`, `/srv/bootfs`, `/srv/proc`, `/srv/net`, `/srv/tmpfs`, `/srv/null`, `/srv/ptyd`, `/srv/sys`, `/srv/nsd`, `/srv/acpi`, the disks and network devices devmgr(8)'s drivers serve, and on an installed system `/srv/fsd`, `/srv/esp` and `/srv/dist`; see services(7).

@node=making title="How a process gets its namespace"

A process is given its namespace by whoever starts it, in its spawn message (svc(6)): mount and bind records, in order, with a connector for each server mounted, which its runtime replays at its first use of a name.
A process whose spawn message has none has an empty namespace.

svcd builds each service's from its manifest: `mount=` and `bind=` records, and `ns=`<name>, which names a namespace template, `/lib/ns/`<name> in the boot image, whose lines become records where it stands.
Each service gets a namespace of its own, not shared with any other, and only the connectors its manifest names.

The templates are in `/lib/ns`.
There is one so far, `/lib/ns/posix`, the namespace a POSIX program expects: the boot image on `/`, the commands on `/bin`, a RAM file system on `/tmp`, the null devices and terminals on `/dev`, and `/proc`, `/net` and `/sys` (see posix(7)).
A template is a script, not data, so it is namespace(6), not ndb(6).

A program spawning a child gives it its own namespace, shared, as the next node describes.

@node=groups title="Namespace groups and nsd"

As Plan 9's `rfork` shares a namespace unless asked not to, a child joins its parent's namespace group, and a `bind` either makes is seen by both.
So a script's `bind` reaches the shell that ran it, and a command run from the console shell sees the shell's mounts.

A group is held by nsd(4), one for the node, as namespace(6) text and the connectors its mount lines name.
nsd publishes the text in memory each member maps read-only, after a sequence number.
Each member keeps its own table, made from the text; before it resolves a name it looks at the sequence, and if the text has changed it builds its table again.
So resolving a name never asks nsd.
A change is made on the member's own table first, then sent to nsd as the new text, with the sequence it was made from; if another member changed the group meanwhile, nsd refuses, and the member catches up and tries again.

A process keeps a table of its own until it first spawns a child; that makes a group, with the process as its first member.
A process that never spawns never talks to nsd, and a process without nsd's post (a manifest's `connect=nsd`) gives its children a copy, as records, instead.
After a fork, the child has its own channel to the group and maps the text again.

Connections are a member's own: each opens its own connection to each server, through a duplicate of the group's connector, since a ring has one producer (see rings(7)).
An open file a child inherits is shared with it through its server, by share(5)'s tokens, since a fid belongs to one connection.

@node=printing title="What ns prints"

ns(1) prints its namespace as namespace(6) text: a `mount` or `bind` line for each member of each union, in the order the members were added, since a later one may need a path an earlier one made.
The first member of a union has no flag; a later one has `-b` if it came before every member added so far and `-a` otherwise; a member that takes creates has `-c` too.
`/proc/`<pid>`/ns`, which procfs(4) gets from nsd, is the same text for the group process <pid> is in.

The text can be run again: rc(1) takes the same lines, so `ns` output rebuilds the namespace in another shell that holds the same connectors.

@node=authority title="Authority"

Because vx-ns runs inside the process, a process can ignore its table and speak 9Px itself, from any fid on any connection it holds, `..` included.
Confinement therefore comes only from the connections: whoever builds a restricted namespace gives the child only the connections it may use, each attached at the root it may see, and the server keeps a client inside its attach root, answering `..` there with the root itself.
A template that leaves out `/srv` and `/net` gives a process nothing it could mount from them, which is why there is no `RFNOMNT`.

# EXAMPLES

The namespace test's manifest, `tests/user/nstest.ndb`, its comment left out, and the namespace `nstest` prints with `ns`'s code, from the test's log.
`/bin` is a union of the boot image's `/bin` and `/boot/bin`, and `/dev` one of the boot image's `/dev` and its `boot/svc` directory, attached by name:

```ndb
service=nstest program=/boot/bin/nstest console restart
arg=first
arg="second arg"
mount=/ srv=bootfs
bind=/bin new=/boot/bin flags=a
mount=/dev srv=bootfs aname=boot/svc flags=a
```

```text
mount /srv/bootfs /
bind /bin /bin
bind -a /boot/bin /bin
bind /dev /dev
mount -a /srv/bootfs /dev boot/svc
```

At the console, a bind and a mount, which every command run after them sees:

```rc
% bind -a /boot/bin /bin; ns | tail -1
bind -a /boot/bin /bin
% mount /srv/bootfs /n; ls /n; ns | tail -1
adm  bin  boot  dev  dist  lib  n  net  proc  rc  srv  sys  tmp
mount /srv/bootfs /n
```

# FILES

: `/lib/ns/*`
  The namespace templates.
: `/srv/<name>`
  A post, as mounts name it.
: `/proc/<pid>/ns`
  The namespace of the group process <pid> is in.

# SEE ALSO

ns(1), rc(1), nsd(4), procfs(4), namespace(6), svc(6), svcd(8), utf(6), walk(5), attach(5), share(5), services(7), posix(7), rings(7); ADR-0009, `docs/02-namespace-swarm.md` §2 and §7, and Plan 9's The Use of Name Spaces in Plan 9 (`sys/doc/names.ms` in 9front's tree)

# BUGS

`/srv` is not a file tree: posts are made only from manifests at boot, `ls /srv` fails, and a program cannot post a service at run time.

Every spawn shares: there is no copied group (`rfork n` in rc, Plan 9's `RFNAMEG`) and no clean one but svcd's, so a script cannot keep its binds to itself.
There is no `newns` command, and no program applies a template but svcd, which takes only its `mount /srv/`<name> and `bind` lines; nothing gives a template `$`<name> variables, so `posix.user` and the per-package namespaces of `docs/02-namespace-swarm.md` §2 and §7 do not exist yet.

nsd is not restarted, and its groups would be lost if it were.
A group's text is at most 16 KiB, a member replays the whole text after any change, and a group's connectors are never let go, so a group takes at most 8 servers over its life.
nsd takes a member's word for which process it is, so `/proc/`<pid>`/ns` can be read, or claimed, by another process until there are capability tokens.

There is no `/env`, `/dev/cons` or `consctl`, and the console is a handle given at spawn (M6 step 6e brings `/env`).
`/fd/0` to `/fd/9` open a copy of the process's descriptor (ADR-0040), but are not in the namespace: `/fd` is not listed, bound or shown by ns(1).

Each process dials its own TCP connection for a `tcp!` mount, and a forked child dials again (M6 step 6d shares one).
